## AJX turns agent retries and mistakes into product usability feedback

Source: Roy Osherove · Show HN — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50399373
Published: 2026-11-06
Channel: hn

AJX is a command-line tool and skill bundle for testing how easily an AI agent can use a product. Its creator, Roy Osherove, introduced it in a first-party Show HN announcement. The problem it examines is agent usability: a product may be usable by people while its command output, documentation or feedback causes an agent to make mistakes and retry.

First, a user supplies a task, product links and a definition of success. An outer harness, the software coordinating the test, starts an inner agent in a chosen environment. Configuration can include the agent tool, model, skills and number of runs. After the agent finishes, the outer harness examines the run, logs and outputs for retries, token use, mistakes and assumptions. It produces a report and a list of requested product improvements.

Osherove gives examples such as missing machine-readable command output and documentation that disagrees with actual behavior. He describes the tool as early and rough, and envisions using its feedback in a continuous delivery loop. These are the developer’s claims and plans; the announcement supplies no independent measurement of improvements.

Why it matters: Product teams building interfaces for agents can use task-level evidence to investigate friction; verify suggested fixes against repeat runs.

